        4
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Punxsutawney%2C_Pennsylvania",
      "category": "etymology",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "The borough was officially founded in 1818, according to the Pennsylvania Department of Transportation Keystone Marker.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        3
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Punxsutawney%2C_Pennsylvania",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "Reverend John Ettwein arrived in 1772 with 241 Christianized Lenape, establishing the first non‑indigenous settlement.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        6
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Punxsutawney%2C_Pennsylvania",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "By the 1850s, mining gave way to factories—brickworks, glassworks, tanneries, and ironworks—shaping Punxsutawney’s industrial scene.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        5
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Punxsutawney%2C_Pennsylvania",
      "category": "economy",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "Every February 2, Punxsutawney hosts Groundhog Day, where Punxsutawney Phil predicts the weather for the nation.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        20
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Punxsutawney%2C_Pennsylvania",
      "category": "culture",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 6,
      "text": "Coal mining, lumber, and railroads drove Punxsutawney’s growth through the 19th and early 20th centuries.",
